{"task_id": "Mbpp/94", "solution": "```python\ndef index_minimum(data: list[tuple[str, int]]) -> str:\n    \"\"\"\n    Given a list of tuples, returns the first value of the tuple with the smallest second value.\n\n    Args:\n        data: A list of tuples, where each tuple contains a string and an integer.\n              Example: [('Rash', 143), ('Manjeet', 200), ('Varsha', 100)]\n\n    Returns:\n        The first value (string) of the tuple that has the smallest second value (integer).\n        In case of ties, the first occurrence in the list is prioritized.\n    \"\"\"\n    if not data:\n        raise ValueError(\"Input list cannot be empty.\")\n\n    # Initialize with the first element's values\n    min_value = data[0][1]\n    result_key = data[0][0]\n\n    # Iterate through the rest of the list to find the minimum\n    for key, value in data[1:]:\n        if value < min_value:\n            min_value = value\n            result_key = key\n\n    return result_key\n```"}
