{"task_id": "Mbpp/116", "solution": "```python\ndef tuple_to_int(data_tuple: tuple[int, ...]) -> int:\n    \"\"\"\n    Converts a tuple of positive integers into a single integer.\n\n    Each integer in the tuple is converted to its string representation,\n    these strings are concatenated, and the resulting string is then\n    converted back into an integer.\n\n    Args:\n        data_tuple: A tuple of integers. Each integer must be non-negative.\n\n    Returns:\n        A single integer formed by concatenating the tuple elements.\n        Returns 0 if the input tuple is empty.\n\n    Raises:\n        TypeError: If the input `data_tuple` is not a tuple.\n        ValueError: If any element in `data_tuple` is not an integer\n                    or is a negative integer.\n\n    Example:\n        >>> tuple_to_int((1, 2, 3))\n        123\n        >>> tuple_to_int((10, 0, 5))\n        1005\n        >>> tuple_to_int(())\n        0\n    \"\"\"\n    if not isinstance(data_tuple, tuple):\n        raise TypeError(\"Input must be a tuple.\")\n\n    if not data_tuple:\n        return 0\n\n    string_parts = []\n    for item in data_tuple:\n        if not isinstance(item, int):\n            raise ValueError(f\"All elements in the tuple must be integers. Found type {type(item).__name__}.\")\n        if item < 0:\n            raise ValueError(\"All elements in the tuple must be positive integers (or zero).\")\n        string_parts.append(str(item))\n\n    return int(\"\".join(string_parts))\n```"}
