{"task_id": "Mbpp/119", "solution": "```python\ndef search(arr: list[int]) -> int:\n    \"\"\"\n    Finds the single unique element in a sorted array.\n    Assumes array is sorted, non-empty, with one unique element and others appearing twice.\n    \"\"\"\n    low, high = 0, len(arr) - 1\n\n    while low < high:\n        mid = (low + high) // 2\n\n        # Determine if the current mid index is even or odd.\n        is_even_index = (mid % 2 == 0)\n\n        if is_even_index:\n            # If mid is even, its pair should be at mid + 1.\n            if arr[mid] == arr[mid + 1]:\n                # If they match, the unique element is to the right of this pair.\n                low = mid + 2\n            else:\n                # If they don't match, arr[mid] is either the unique element\n                # or the unique element is to its left.\n                high = mid\n        else: # mid is odd\n            # If mid is odd, its pair should be at mid - 1.\n            if arr[mid] == arr[mid - 1]:\n                # If they match, the unique element is to the right of this pair.\n                low = mid + 1\n            else:\n                # If they don't match, arr[mid] is either the unique element\n                # or the unique element is to its left.\n                high = mid\n\n    # When the loop terminates, low == high, and this index points to the unique element.\n    return arr[low]\n```"}
{"task_id": "Mbpp/119", "solution": "def search(array: list[int]) -> int:\n    \"\"\"\n    Identifies the single unique element in a sorted array where all other elements appear exactly twice.\n    Employs a binary search algorithm for O(log n) performance.\n    \"\"\"\n    left, right = 0, len(array) - 1\n\n    while left < right:\n        mid = (left + right) // 2\n        # Ensure 'mid' is at an even index to properly check pairs (mid, mid+1).\n        if mid % 2 == 1:\n            mid -= 1\n\n        # If array[mid] and array[mid+1] form a pair, the unique element is to their right.\n        if array[mid] == array[mid + 1]:\n            left = mid + 2\n        # Otherwise, the unique element is at or before array[mid].\n        else:\n            right = mid\n\n    # When the loop terminates, 'left' (which equals 'right') points to the unique element.\n    return array[left]"}
{"task_id": "Mbpp/120", "solution": "```python\ndef max_product_tuple(data: list[tuple[int | float, int | float]]) -> int | float:\n    \"\"\"Calculates the maximum absolute product from a list of number pairs.\n    Returns 0 if the input list is empty.\n    \"\"\"\n    if not data:\n        return 0\n\n    max_abs_product = 0.0\n\n    for num1, num2 in data:\n        current_product = num1 * num2\n        max_abs_product = max(max_abs_product, abs(current_product))\n            \n    return max_abs_product\n```"}
