{"task_id": "Mbpp/125", "solution": "```python\ndef find_length(binary_string: str) -> int:\n    \"\"\"Calculates the maximum difference between 0s and 1s in any substring.\n    0s are counted as +1, 1s as -1, and Kadane's algorithm is applied.\n    \"\"\"\n    transformed_array = []\n    for char in binary_string:\n        if char == '0':\n            transformed_array.append(1)\n        else:  # char == '1'\n            transformed_array.append(-1)\n\n    max_so_far = 0\n    current_max = 0\n\n    for x in transformed_array:\n        current_max += x\n        if current_max < 0:\n            current_max = 0\n        if current_max > max_so_far:\n            max_so_far = current_max\n\n    return max_so_far\n```"}
{"task_id": "Mbpp/126", "solution": "import math\n\ndef sum(num1: int, num2: int) -> int:\n    \"\"\"\n    Calculates the sum of common divisors of two given positive integers.\n\n    Args:\n        num1: The first positive integer.\n        num2: The second positive integer.\n\n    Returns:\n        The sum of the common divisors.\n\n    Raises:\n        ValueError: If num1 or num2 are not positive integers.\n    \"\"\"\n    if not isinstance(num1, int) or num1 <= 0 or \\\n       not isinstance(num2, int) or num2 <= 0:\n        raise ValueError(\"Inputs must be positive integers.\")\n\n    # Calculate GCD using Euclidean algorithm\n    a, b = num1, num2\n    while b:\n        a, b = b, a % b\n    common_gcd = a\n\n    # Find divisors of the GCD\n    divs = []\n    for i in range(1, int(math.sqrt(common_gcd)) + 1):\n        if common_gcd % i == 0:\n            divs.append(i)\n            if i * i != common_gcd:\n                divs.append(common_gcd // i)\n    \n    # Sum the common divisors\n    return sum(divs)"}
{"task_id": "Mbpp/125", "solution": "```python\ndef find_length(s: str) -> int:\n    \"\"\"\n    Calculates the maximum difference between the number of '0's and '1's\n    in any substring of the given binary string.\n\n    This function uses a variant of Kadane's algorithm to achieve O(n) time complexity\n    and O(1) space complexity. It transforms the problem into finding the maximum\n    subarray sum by treating '0' as 1 and '1' as -1.\n\n    Args:\n        s: The input binary string consisting of '0's and '1's.\n\n    Returns:\n        An integer representing the maximum difference.\n\n    Raises:\n        ValueError: If the input string is None or contains non-binary characters.\n\n    Examples:\n        >>> find_length(\"11000010001\")\n        6\n        >>> find_length(\"0000\")\n        4\n        >>> find_length(\"1111\")\n        -1\n        >>> find_length(\"\")\n        0\n        >>> find_length(\"010101\")\n        1\n    \"\"\"\n    if s is None:\n        raise ValueError(\"Input string cannot be None.\")\n    if not all(char in '01' for char in s):\n        raise ValueError(\"Input string must contain only '0's and '1's.\")\n\n    if not s:\n        return 0\n\n    # Initialize max_so_far and current_max for Kadane's algorithm.\n    # The first element's transformed value initializes these.\n    # If s[0] is '0', val = 1. If s[0] is '1', val = -1.\n    if s[0] == '0':\n        first_val = 1\n    else: # s[0] == '1'\n        first_val = -1\n\n    max_so_far = first_val\n    current_max = first_val\n\n    # Iterate from the second character to perform Kadane's algorithm.\n    for i in range(1, len(s)):\n        if s[i] == '0':\n            val = 1\n        else: # s[i] == '1'\n            val = -1\n        \n        # current_max is the maximum sum ending at the current position.\n        # It's either the current element itself or the current element plus the previous sum.\n        current_max = max(val, current_max + val)\n        \n        # max_so_far keeps track of the overall maximum sum found so far.\n        max_so_far = max(max_so_far, current_max)\n\n    return max_so_far\n```"}
