{"task_id": "Mbpp/244", "solution": "```python\nimport math\n\ndef next_Perfect_Square(number: int) -> int:\n    \"\"\"\n    Calculates the smallest perfect square strictly greater than the input number.\n    Assumes input is an integer; no validation or error handling for non-integers.\n    \"\"\"\n    if number < 0:\n        # For negative inputs, the smallest perfect square strictly greater than it is 0.\n        return 0\n\n    # Calculate the integer square root of the number.\n    # For example, math.isqrt(35) is 5, math.isqrt(36) is 6.\n    integer_sqrt = math.isqrt(number)\n\n    # The next perfect square will be the square of (integer_sqrt + 1).\n    # If number is 35 (sqrt=5), next root is 6, result is 36.\n    # If number is 36 (sqrt=6), next root is 7, result is 49.\n    next_root = integer_sqrt + 1\n    return next_root * next_root\n```"}
{"task_id": "Mbpp/244", "solution": "```python\ndef next_Perfect_Square(number):\n    \"\"\"\n    Finds the next perfect square strictly greater than the given number.\n    Handles negative numbers by returning the smallest non-negative perfect square.\n    \"\"\"\n    if number < 0:\n        return 0\n    \n    # Calculate the integer part of the square root using the exponentiation operator.\n    root = int(number**0.5)\n    \n    # The next perfect square is found by squaring the integer immediately after 'root'.\n    # This covers cases where 'number' is already a perfect square or a non-square.\n    next_root_candidate = root + 1\n    return next_root_candidate * next_root_candidate\n```"}
{"task_id": "Mbpp/245", "solution": "```python\ndef max_sum(arr: list[int]) -> int:\n    \"\"\"\n    Calculates the maximum sum of a bitonic subsequence in the given array.\n    A bitonic sequence is first strictly increasing, then strictly decreasing.\n    \"\"\"\n    n = len(arr)\n\n    if n == 0:\n        return 0\n    if n == 1:\n        return arr[0]\n\n    lis_sums = [0] * n\n    for i in range(n):\n        lis_sums[i] = arr[i]\n        for j in range(i):\n            if arr[i] > arr[j]:\n                lis_sums[i] = max(lis_sums[i], lis_sums[j] + arr[i])\n\n    lds_sums = [0] * n\n    for i in range(n - 1, -1, -1):\n        lds_sums[i] = arr[i]\n        for j in range(n - 1, i, -1):\n            if arr[i] > arr[j]:\n                lds_sums[i] = max(lds_sums[i], lds_sums[j] + arr[i])\n\n    max_bitonic_sum = 0\n    for i in range(n):\n        # The peak element arr[i] is counted in both LIS and LDS sums, so subtract it once.\n        current_bitonic_sum = lis_sums[i] + lds_sums[i] - arr[i]\n        max_bitonic_sum = max(max_bitonic_sum, current_bitonic_sum)\n        \n    return max_bitonic_sum\n```"}
{"task_id": "Mbpp/245", "solution": "```python\ndef max_sum(arr: list[int]) -> int:\n    \"\"\"Calculates the maximum sum of a bitonic subsequence.\n    A bitonic subsequence first strictly increases and then strictly decreases.\n    \"\"\"\n    n = len(arr)\n\n    if n == 0:\n        return 0\n    if n == 1:\n        return arr[0]\n\n    def _calculate_msis(current_arr):\n        \"\"\"Calculates Maximum Sum Increasing Subsequence ending at each position.\"\"\"\n        msis = list(current_arr)\n        for i in range(1, len(current_arr)):\n            for j in range(i):\n                if current_arr[i] > current_arr[j] and msis[i] < msis[j] + current_arr[i]:\n                    msis[i] = msis[j] + current_arr[i]\n        return msis\n\n    def _calculate_msds(current_arr):\n        \"\"\"Calculates Maximum Sum Decreasing Subsequence starting at each position.\"\"\"\n        msds = list(current_arr)\n        for i in range(len(current_arr) - 2, -1, -1):\n            for j in range(len(current_arr) - 1, i, -1):\n                if current_arr[i] > current_arr[j] and msds[i] < msds[j] + current_arr[i]:\n                    msds[i] = msds[j] + current_arr[i]\n        return msds\n\n    msis_values = _calculate_msis(arr)\n    msds_values = _calculate_msds(arr)\n\n    # Initialize max_sum to 0, which correctly handles arrays with all negative bitonic sums.\n    max_sum = 0\n    for i in range(n):\n        # Subtract arr[i] because it's counted in both MSIS and MSDS parts.\n        current_bitonic_sum = msis_values[i] + msds_values[i] - arr[i]\n        if current_bitonic_sum > max_sum:\n            max_sum = current_bitonic_sum\n    \n    return max_sum\n```"}
{"task_id": "Mbpp/247", "solution": "```python\ndef lps(s: str) -> int:\n    \"\"\"Calculates the length of the longest palindromic subsequence in a string.\n    Uses dynamic programming with O(n^2) time and space complexity.\"\"\"\n    n = len(s)\n    if n == 0:\n        return 0\n\n    # dp[i][j] stores the length of the longest palindromic subsequence of s[i...j]\n    dp = [[0] * n for _ in range(n)]\n\n    # All single characters are palindromes of length 1\n    for i in range(n):\n        dp[i][i] = 1\n\n    # Fill the table for lengths from 2 to n\n    for current_len in range(2, n + 1):\n        for i in range(n - current_len + 1):\n            j = i + current_len - 1\n            if s[i] == s[j]:\n                # If characters match, add 2 to the LPS of the inner substring\n                # dp[i+1][j-1] handles the case where the inner substring is empty (length < 2)\n                dp[i][j] = 2 + dp[i + 1][j - 1]\n            else:\n                # If characters don't match, take the maximum of excluding s[i] or s[j]\n                dp[i][j] = max(dp[i + 1][j], dp[i][j - 1])\n\n    return dp[0][n - 1]\n```"}
