{"task_id": "Mbpp/255", "solution": "```python\nfrom typing import List, Tuple, Any\n\ndef combinations_colors(elements: List[Any], n: int) -> List[Tuple[Any, ...]]:\n    \"\"\"Generates all combinations with repetition from elements of a given length.\n    Elements in a combination are ordered based on their appearance in the input list.\"\"\"\n    result: List[Tuple[Any, ...]] = []\n\n    def _generate(current_combination: List[Any], start_index: int) -> None:\n        if len(current_combination) == n:\n            result.append(tuple(current_combination))\n            return\n\n        for i in range(start_index, len(elements)):\n            current_combination.append(elements[i])\n            _generate(current_combination, i)\n            current_combination.pop()\n\n    _generate([], 0)\n    return result\n```"}
{"task_id": "Mbpp/255", "solution": "from typing import List, Tuple, Any\n\ndef combinations_colors(data_list: List[Any], n: int) -> List[Tuple[Any, ...]]:\n    \"\"\"\n    Generates all unique combinations with repetition of a specified length 'n'\n    from a given list of elements.\n    \"\"\"\n    if n < 0 or not data_list:\n        return []\n    if n == 0:\n        return [()]\n\n    result: List[Tuple[Any, ...]] = []\n    num_elements = len(data_list)\n\n    def _backtrack(current_combination: List[Any], start_index: int):\n        if len(current_combination) == n:\n            result.append(tuple(current_combination))\n            return\n\n        for i in range(start_index, num_elements):\n            current_combination.append(data_list[i])\n            _backtrack(current_combination, i)\n            current_combination.pop()\n\n    _backtrack([], 0)\n    return result"}
{"task_id": "Mbpp/256", "solution": "```python\ndef count_Primes_nums(num: int) -> int:\n    \"\"\"\n    Counts prime numbers strictly less than the given non-negative number.\n    It uses a nested helper function for primality testing.\n    \"\"\"\n\n    def _is_prime(n: int) -> bool:\n        \"\"\"\n        Checks if a given number n is prime.\n        Returns True if n is prime, False otherwise.\n        \"\"\"\n        if n < 2:\n            return False\n        i = 2\n        while i * i <= n:\n            if n % i == 0:\n                return False\n            i += 1\n        return True\n\n    count = 0\n    # Iterate through numbers from 2 up to, but not including, 'num'\n    for i in range(2, num):\n        if _is_prime(i):\n            count += 1\n    return count\n```"}
{"task_id": "Mbpp/256", "solution": "```python\ndef count_Primes_nums(n: int) -> int:\n    \"\"\"\n    Returns the number of prime numbers strictly less than n.\n    This function implements the Sieve of Eratosthenes.\n    \"\"\"\n    if n <= 2:\n        return 0\n\n    # Initialize a boolean list where primes[i] is True if i is potentially prime.\n    # We check numbers up to n-1, so the list size is n.\n    primes = [True] * n\n    primes[0] = primes[1] = False # 0 and 1 are not prime numbers.\n\n    # Apply the Sieve of Eratosthenes algorithm.\n    # Iterate from 2 up to the square root of n.\n    for p in range(2, int(n**0.5) + 1):\n        if primes[p]:\n            # Mark multiples of p as not prime, starting from p*p.\n            for multiple in range(p*p, n, p):\n                primes[multiple] = False\n    \n    # Count the number of True values in the primes list.\n    return sum(1 for is_prime in primes if is_prime)\n```"}
