{"task_id": "Mbpp/414", "solution": "```python\nfrom typing import Sequence\n\ndef overlapping(seq1: Sequence, seq2: Sequence) -> bool:\n    \"\"\"Checks if any element from `seq1` is also present in `seq2`.\n    Returns `True` if at least one common element is found, `False` otherwise.\n    \"\"\"\n    if not seq1 or not seq2:\n        return False\n\n    # Convert the smaller sequence to a set for O(1) average lookup time.\n    if len(seq1) <= len(seq2):\n        set1 = set(seq1)\n        for item in seq2:\n            if item in set1:\n                return True\n    else:\n        set2 = set(seq2)\n        for item in seq1:\n            if item in set2:\n                return True\n    return False\n```"}
{"task_id": "Mbpp/415", "solution": "```python\nfrom typing import List, Tuple\n\ndef max_Product(numbers: List[int]) -> Tuple[int, int]:\n    \"\"\"\n    Finds the pair of numbers with the highest product in a given array.\n    Returns (0, 0) if the input list has fewer than two elements.\n    \"\"\"\n    if len(numbers) < 2:\n        return (0, 0)\n\n    # Initialize variables to track the two largest and two smallest numbers.\n    max1 = float('-inf')\n    max2 = float('-inf')\n    min1 = float('inf')\n    min2 = float('inf')\n\n    for num in numbers:\n        # Update the two largest numbers found so far\n        if num > max1:\n            max2 = max1\n            max1 = num\n        elif num > max2:\n            max2 = num\n\n        # Update the two smallest numbers found so far\n        if num < min1:\n            min2 = min1\n            min1 = num\n        elif num < min2:\n            min2 = num\n    \n    # Compare the product of the two largest numbers with the product of the two smallest numbers.\n    product1 = max1 * max2\n    product2 = min1 * min2\n\n    if product1 >= product2:\n        return (int(max2), int(max1))\n    else:\n        return (int(min1), int(min2))\n```"}
{"task_id": "Mbpp/415", "solution": "```python\ndef max_Product(numbers: list[int]) -> tuple[int, int]:\n    \"\"\"\n    Finds two numbers from a list whose product is the highest.\n    Assumes input is a list of at least two integers.\n    \"\"\"\n    max1 = float('-inf')\n    max2 = float('-inf')\n    min1 = float('inf')\n    min2 = float('inf')\n\n    for num in numbers:\n        if num > max1:\n            max2 = max1\n            max1 = num\n        elif num > max2:\n            max2 = num\n\n        if num < min1:\n            min2 = min1\n            min1 = num\n        elif num < min2:\n            min2 = num\n\n    if max1 * max2 >= min1 * min2:\n        return (int(max2), int(max1))\n    else:\n        return (int(min1), int(min2))\n```"}
