{"task_id": "Mbpp/468", "solution": "```python\ndef max_product(numbers: list[int]) -> int:\n    \"\"\"Calculates the maximum product of an increasing subsequence.\"\"\"\n    \n    n = len(numbers)\n    if n == 0:\n        return 0\n    if n == 1:\n        return numbers[0]\n\n    # dp[i] stores the maximum product of an increasing subsequence ending at index i.\n    dp = list(numbers) \n\n    for i in range(1, n):\n        for j in range(i):\n            if numbers[i] > numbers[j]:\n                # Update dp[i] if extending a subsequence ending at j yields a larger product.\n                dp[i] = max(dp[i], dp[j] * numbers[i])\n    \n    return max(dp)\n```"}
{"task_id": "Mbpp/468", "solution": "def max_product(arr: list[int]) -> int:\n    \"\"\"\n    Calculates the maximum product of an increasing subsequence within the input array.\n    Uses dynamic programming to handle positive, negative, and zero elements efficiently.\n    \"\"\"\n    if not arr:\n        return 0\n\n    n = len(arr)\n    dp_max = [0] * n  # Max product of increasing subsequence ending at index i.\n    dp_min = [0] * n  # Min product of increasing subsequence ending at index i.\n    \n    # Initialize overall_max_product to handle single-element subsequences correctly,\n    # especially for arrays with only negative numbers where the max product might be a single negative number.\n    overall_max_product = max(arr) \n\n    for i in range(n):\n        dp_max[i] = arr[i]\n        dp_min[i] = arr[i]\n\n        for j in range(i):\n            if arr[i] > arr[j]:\n                if arr[i] >= 0:\n                    dp_max[i] = max(dp_max[i], dp_max[j] * arr[i])\n                    dp_min[i] = min(dp_min[i], dp_min[j] * arr[i])\n                else: # arr[i] < 0, a negative multiplier\n                    dp_max[i] = max(dp_max[i], dp_min[j] * arr[i]) # negative * negative -> positive\n                    dp_min[i] = min(dp_min[i], dp_max[j] * arr[i]) # positive * negative -> negative\n        \n        overall_max_product = max(overall_max_product, dp_max[i])\n        \n    return overall_max_product"}
