{"task_id": "Mbpp/471", "solution": "```python\ndef find_remainder(arr: list[int], n: int) -> int:\n    \"\"\"\n    Calculates the product of all elements in an array modulo n.\n    This function avoids integer overflow by applying modulo at each step.\n    \"\"\"\n    if not isinstance(n, int) or n <= 0:\n        raise ValueError(\"n must be a positive integer.\")\n\n    product = 1\n    for element in arr:\n        if not isinstance(element, int):\n            raise TypeError(\"All elements in the array must be integers.\")\n        product = (product * element) % n\n    return product\n```\n"}
{"task_id": "Mbpp/472", "solution": "```python\ndef check_Consecutive(numbers: list) -> bool:\n    \"\"\"\n    Determines if all numbers in a list are consecutive after sorting.\n    Returns True for empty or single-element lists.\n    \"\"\"\n    if len(numbers) <= 1:\n        return True\n\n    sorted_numbers = sorted(numbers)\n\n    for i in range(1, len(sorted_numbers)):\n        if not (sorted_numbers[i] - sorted_numbers[i-1] == 1):\n            return False\n    return True\n```"}
{"task_id": "Mbpp/472", "solution": "```python\ndef check_Consecutive(numbers_list: list[int | float]) -> bool:\n    \"\"\"\n    Checks if a list of numbers contains consecutive elements.\n    Considers unique sorted elements to determine consecutiveness.\n    \"\"\"\n    if len(numbers_list) <= 1:\n        return True\n\n    # Convert to set to handle duplicates as per business rule BR1, then sort the unique elements.\n    sorted_unique_numbers = sorted(list(set(numbers_list)))\n\n    # Iterate through the sorted unique list and check for consecutiveness.\n    for i in range(len(sorted_unique_numbers) - 1):\n        if sorted_unique_numbers[i] + 1 != sorted_unique_numbers[i+1]:\n            return False\n    return True\n```"}
{"task_id": "Mbpp/473", "solution": "def tuple_intersection(list1: list[tuple], list2: list[tuple]) -> set[tuple]:\n    \"\"\"\n    Finds the intersection of two lists of tuples, ignoring element order within tuples.\n    Returns a set of canonical (sorted) tuples found in both input lists.\n    \"\"\"\n    canonical_set1 = {tuple(sorted(t)) for t in list1}\n    canonical_set2 = {tuple(sorted(t)) for t in list2}\n    return canonical_set1.intersection(canonical_set2)"}
{"task_id": "Mbpp/473", "solution": "```python\nfrom typing import List, Tuple, Set, Any\n\ndef _normalize_tuple(t: Tuple[Any, ...]) -> Tuple[Any, ...]:\n    \"\"\"Normalizes a tuple by sorting its elements.\n    This makes (3, 4) and (4, 3) both canonical to (3, 4).\"\"\"\n    return tuple(sorted(t))\n\ndef tuple_intersection(list1: List[Tuple[Any, ...]], list2: List[Tuple[Any, ...]]) -> Set[Tuple[Any, ...]]:\n    \"\"\"Finds the intersection of elements in two given tuple lists, irrespective of their order within the tuples.\n    Returns a set of tuples with elements sorted for canonical representation.\"\"\"\n    normalized_set1 = {_normalize_tuple(t) for t in list1}\n    normalized_set2 = {_normalize_tuple(t) for t in list2}\n    return normalized_set1.intersection(normalized_set2)\n```"}
