{"task_id": "Mbpp/597", "solution": "```python\ndef find_kth(arr1: list, arr2: list, k: int) -> any:\n    \"\"\"Finds the k-th smallest element from two sorted arrays.\n    This implementation uses a recursive binary search-like approach for O(log(min(m, n))) time complexity.\n    \"\"\"\n\n    def _find_kth_recursive(a: list, b: list, current_k: int) -> any:\n        \"\"\"Recursive helper to find the k-th smallest element.\n        It assumes k is within bounds of the combined lengths of the original arrays.\n        \"\"\"\n        len_a, len_b = len(a), len(b)\n\n        # Optimize by ensuring 'a' is always the shorter array for recursion efficiency.\n        if len_a > len_b:\n            return _find_kth_recursive(b, a, current_k)\n\n        # Base cases:\n        # If 'a' is empty, the k-th element must be in 'b'.\n        if len_a == 0:\n            return b[current_k - 1]\n        # If we are looking for the 1st element, it's the minimum of the first elements.\n        if current_k == 1:\n            return min(a[0], b[0])\n\n        # Divide and conquer strategy:\n        # Try to discard roughly k/2 elements.\n        # 'part_a' elements to consider from array 'a'.\n        # 'part_b' elements to consider from array 'b'.\n        part_a = min(current_k // 2, len_a)\n        part_b = current_k - part_a # Ensures part_a + part_b == current_k\n\n        # Compare elements at the partition points.\n        if a[part_a - 1] < b[part_b - 1]:\n            # If a's partition element is smaller, it means all elements before it in 'a'\n            # and 'part_a' itself can be discarded. Recurse on the rest of 'a' and all of 'b'.\n            return _find_kth_recursive(a[part_a:], b, current_k - part_a)\n        else:\n            # If b's partition element is smaller or equal, discard elements before it in 'b'.\n            # Recurse on all of 'a' and the rest of 'b'.\n            return _find_kth_recursive(a, b[part_b:], current_k - part_b)\n\n    # The public function initiates the recursive process.\n    # CRITICAL REQUIREMENT: No exception for k out of bounds is added here,\n    # trusting caller's input as per specification.\n    return _find_kth_recursive(arr1, arr2, k)\n```"}
{"task_id": "Mbpp/597", "solution": "```python\ndef find_kth(arr1: list[int], arr2: list[int], k: int) -> int:\n    \"\"\"\n    Finds the k-th smallest element from two given sorted arrays.\n    Uses a simple two-pointer (merge-like) approach.\n    \"\"\"\n    len1, len2 = len(arr1), len(arr2)\n    \n    i, j = 0, 0\n    current_k = 0\n    result = 0 # This variable will hold the k-th element\n\n    while i < len1 and j < len2:\n        current_k += 1\n        if arr1[i] < arr2[j]:\n            result = arr1[i]\n            i += 1\n        else:\n            result = arr2[j]\n            j += 1\n        if current_k == k:\n            return result\n\n    while i < len1:\n        current_k += 1\n        result = arr1[i]\n        i += 1\n        if current_k == k:\n            return result\n\n    while j < len2:\n        current_k += 1\n        result = arr2[j]\n        j += 1\n        if current_k == k:\n            return result\n    \n    # According to problem constraints (k is valid), this point should not be reached without returning.\n    # If it is reached, it implies an invalid k, but we trust caller's inputs.\n    return result\n```"}
