{"task_id": "Mbpp/598", "solution": "```python\ndef armstrong_number(number: int) -> bool:\n    \"\"\"\n    Checks if the given number is an Armstrong number.\n    An Armstrong number equals the sum of its digits each raised to the power of the total number of digits.\n    \"\"\"\n    if number < 10:\n        return True\n\n    num_str = str(number)\n    num_digits = len(num_str)\n    sum_of_powers = 0\n\n    for digit_char in num_str:\n        digit = int(digit_char)\n        sum_of_powers += pow(digit, num_digits)\n\n    return sum_of_powers == number\n```"}
{"task_id": "Mbpp/598", "solution": "```python\ndef armstrong_number(number):\n    \"\"\"\n    Checks if a number is an Armstrong number.\n    An Armstrong number is the sum of its own digits each raised to the power of the number of digits.\n    \"\"\"\n    \n    # Armstrong numbers are typically defined for positive integers; check based on absolute value.\n    abs_number = abs(number)\n    \n    # Convert the number to a string to determine the number of digits and access individual digits.\n    str_abs_number = str(abs_number)\n    num_digits = len(str_abs_number)\n    \n    sum_of_powers = 0\n    for digit_char in str_abs_number:\n        digit = int(digit_char)\n        sum_of_powers += digit ** num_digits\n        \n    return sum_of_powers == abs_number\n```"}
