{"task_id": "Mbpp/618", "solution": "```python\ndef div_list(list1, list2):\n    \"\"\"\n    Divides elements of the first list by corresponding elements of the second list.\n\n    Args:\n        list1: The numerator list, containing integers or floats.\n        list2: The denominator list, containing integers or floats.\n\n    Returns:\n        A new list of floats, where result[i] = list1[i] / list2[i].\n\n    Raises:\n        TypeError: If list1 or list2 are not lists, or if their elements are not numbers.\n        ValueError: If list1 and list2 have different lengths.\n        ZeroDivisionError: If any element in list2 is zero.\n    \"\"\"\n    if not isinstance(list1, list) or not isinstance(list2, list):\n        raise TypeError(\"Both inputs must be lists.\")\n\n    if len(list1) != len(list2):\n        raise ValueError(\"Input lists must have the same length.\")\n\n    results = []\n    for i in range(len(list1)):\n        num = list1[i]\n        den = list2[i]\n\n        if not isinstance(num, (int, float)) or not isinstance(den, (int, float)):\n            raise TypeError(f\"List elements must be numbers. Found {type(num)} and {type(den)} at index {i}.\")\n\n        if den == 0:\n            raise ZeroDivisionError(f\"Division by zero at index {i}.\")\n\n        results.append(float(num) / float(den))\n    return results\n```\n"}
{"task_id": "Mbpp/620", "solution": "```python\ndef largest_subset(numbers: list) -> int:\n    \"\"\"\n    Finds the size of the largest divisible subset from a given list of numbers.\n    The function assumes valid input as per critical requirements.\n    \"\"\"\n    if not numbers:\n        return 0\n\n    # Sort the numbers to simplify divisibility checks and apply dynamic programming.\n    numbers.sort()\n\n    n = len(numbers)\n    # dp[i] stores the size of the largest divisible subset ending with numbers[i].\n    dp = [1] * n\n\n    for i in range(n):\n        for j in range(i):\n            if numbers[i] % numbers[j] == 0:\n                dp[i] = max(dp[i], dp[j] + 1)\n    \n    return max(dp)\n```"}
{"task_id": "Mbpp/620", "solution": "```python\nfrom typing import List\n\ndef largest_subset(numbers: List[int]) -> int:\n    \"\"\"\n    Finds the size of the largest subset of a list of numbers\n    so that every pair is divisible.\n    \"\"\"\n    if not numbers:\n        return 0\n\n    # Sort the numbers to enable dynamic programming for building divisible chains.\n    # The sorting order helps in processing numbers in a consistent sequence.\n    sorted_numbers = sorted(numbers)\n    n = len(sorted_numbers)\n    \n    # dp[i] stores the size of the largest divisible subset ending with sorted_numbers[i].\n    dp = [1] * n\n\n    for i in range(n):\n        for j in range(i):\n            num_i = sorted_numbers[i]\n            num_j = sorted_numbers[j]\n\n            # Determine if num_i and num_j form a divisible pair based on the rule:\n            # (a % b == 0) or (b % a == 0).\n            is_divisible_pair = False\n            if num_j == 0:\n                # If num_j is 0.\n                # If num_i is also 0, then (0,0) is divisible (0%0 is typically 0).\n                # If num_i is not 0, then (0, num_i). 0 % num_i == 0 (True), num_i % 0 (Error).\n                # By the \"either/or\" rule, this is considered a divisible pair because 0 % num_i == 0.\n                is_divisible_pair = True\n            elif num_i == 0:\n                # If num_i is 0 (and num_j is not 0, as handled by previous 'if').\n                # Pair (num_j, 0). 0 % num_j == 0 (True), num_j % 0 (Error).\n                # By the \"either/or\" rule, this is considered a divisible pair because 0 % num_j == 0.\n                is_divisible_pair = True\n            else:\n                # Both numbers are non-zero. Check divisibility using absolute values.\n                # Since the list is sorted, abs(num_j) might not be strictly less than abs(num_i)\n                # (e.g., -8, -4). So we check both directions.\n                is_divisible_pair = (abs(num_i) % abs(num_j) == 0) or (abs(num_j) % abs(num_i) == 0)\n\n            if is_divisible_pair:\n                dp[i] = max(dp[i], dp[j] + 1)\n    \n    # The maximum value in the dp array is the size of the largest divisible subset.\n    # If dp is empty (e.g., if initial 'numbers' was empty), max() would raise an error.\n    # However, 'numbers' being empty is handled at the beginning, so dp will always have at least one element.\n    return max(dp)\n\n```"}
{"task_id": "Mbpp/622", "solution": "```python\ndef get_median(list1: list[float | int], list2: list[float | int], n: int) -> float:\n    \"\"\"\n    Calculates the median of two sorted lists of the same size n.\n    It finds the two middle elements of the conceptually merged list and returns their average.\n    \"\"\"\n    if n == 0:\n        return 0.0\n\n    i = 0  # Pointer for list1\n    j = 0  # Pointer for list2\n    m1 = -1  # Stores the (n-1)-th element (0-indexed)\n    m2 = -1  # Stores the n-th element (0-indexed)\n\n    # Iterate n+1 times to find the two middle elements of the combined 2n-sized list.\n    # m2 will eventually hold the n-th element, and m1 the (n-1)-th element.\n    for _ in range(n + 1):\n        m1 = m2  # Store the previous candidate for m2 in m1\n\n        if i == n:  # If list1 is exhausted, take from list2\n            m2 = list2[j]\n            j += 1\n        elif j == n:  # If list2 is exhausted, take from list1\n            m2 = list1[i]\n            i += 1\n        elif list1[i] <= list2[j]:  # Compare elements and take the smaller one\n            m2 = list1[i]\n            i += 1\n        else:\n            m2 = list2[j]\n            j += 1\n    return (float(m1) + float(m2)) / 2.0\n```"}
{"task_id": "Mbpp/622", "solution": "def get_median(list1: list[float], list2: list[float], n: int) -> float:\n    \"\"\"\n    Calculates the median of two sorted lists of size n.\n    The median is the average of the n-th and (n+1)-th elements in the combined sequence.\n    \"\"\"\n    i = 0  # Pointer for list1\n    j = 0  # Pointer for list2\n    m1 = 0.0 # To store the n-th element\n    m2 = 0.0 # To store the (n+1)-th element\n\n    # Iterate to find the n-th and (n+1)-th elements from the combined sorted sequence.\n    for count in range(n + 1):\n        if i == n: # list1 is exhausted\n            m1 = m2\n            m2 = float(list2[j])\n            j += 1\n        elif j == n: # list2 is exhausted\n            m1 = m2\n            m2 = float(list1[i])\n            i += 1\n        elif list1[i] <= list2[j]:\n            m1 = m2\n            m2 = float(list1[i])\n            i += 1\n        else: # list2[j] is smaller\n            m1 = m2\n            m2 = float(list2[j])\n            j += 1\n    \n    return (m1 + m2) / 2.0"}
