{"task_id": "Mbpp/755", "solution": "```python\nclass EmptyListError(ValueError):\n    \"\"\"Raised when the input list is empty.\"\"\"\n    pass\n\nclass NotEnoughDistinctElementsError(ValueError):\n    \"\"\"Raised when the input list has fewer than two distinct elements.\"\"\"\n    pass\n\ndef second_smallest(numbers):\n    \"\"\"\n    Finds the second smallest distinct number in a list.\n\n    Args:\n        numbers: A list of integers or floats.\n\n    Returns:\n        The second smallest distinct number.\n\n    Raises:\n        EmptyListError: If the input list is empty.\n        NotEnoughDistinctElementsError: If the list contains fewer than two distinct elements.\n    \"\"\"\n    if not numbers:\n        raise EmptyListError(\"Input list cannot be empty.\")\n\n    unique_numbers = sorted(list(set(numbers)))\n\n    if len(unique_numbers) < 2:\n        raise NotEnoughDistinctElementsError(\"List must contain at least two distinct elements.\")\n\n    return unique_numbers[1]\n```"}
{"task_id": "Mbpp/757", "solution": "```python\ndef count_reverse_pairs(string_list: list[str]) -> int:\n    \"\"\"\n    Counts pairs of strings where one is the reverse of the other, excluding self-reversing strings.\n    Each unique instance of such a pair is counted.\n    \"\"\"\n    seen_strings = set()\n    pairs_count = 0\n\n    for s in string_list:\n        reversed_s = s[::-1]\n        # Count if the reversed string has been seen AND the string is not its own reverse.\n        if reversed_s in seen_strings and s != reversed_s:\n            pairs_count += 1\n        seen_strings.add(s)\n\n    return pairs_count\n```"}
